Project Archimedes: A Self-Assembling Modular Orbital Habitat Based on Autonomous Small-Satellite Swarms

Mathematical Foundations, Architecture, and Feasibility

ouadi Maakoul 


Abstract

The construction of large pressurised habitats in low Earth orbit has, since the dawn of the space age, followed a single paradigm: fabricate large, pre‑integrated modules on Earth, launch them individually on heavy‑lift vehicles, and assemble them in orbit through human extravehicular activity. This approach imposes severe constraints on module dimensions, launch reliability, and cost, and fundamentally limits the scalability of orbital infrastructure. This dissertation presents a complete, mathematically rigorous, and architecturally closed framework—Project Archimedes—for a radically different approach: the autonomous in‑orbit assembly of a 400 m³ human‑rated habitat from approximately 500 small, identical, intelligent tiles (each ≤12 U CubeSat, ≤12.5 kg) that navigate, dock, and reconfigure themselves under the guidance of a hierarchical artificial intelligence.

The work makes seven principal contributions. First, it develops a formal graph‑theoretic model of a swarm‑assembled structure, in which the habitat is represented as a time‑varying dynamic graph with separate mechanical, communication, and fluid‑transfer topologies. Second, it establishes a co‑design methodology for task assignment and collision‑free trajectory generation, employing a robust Consensus‑Based Bundle Algorithm (CBBA) with barrier‑augmented costs and sequential convex programming, and it proves convergence under bounded packet loss. Third, the stochastic nature of assembly is rigorously captured as a Decentralised Partially Observable Markov Decision Process (Dec‑POMDP), solved via a hierarchically decomposed Dec‑MCTS‑SP planner augmented with graph neural network policy priors, for which a probabilistic completeness proof is provided together with an asymptotic complexity bound demonstrating tractability on embedded hardware. Fourth, a suite of nonsmooth control barrier functions is derived that simultaneously enforces algebraic connectivity, collision avoidance, and docking safety through a hierarchical feasibility architecture, and that stabilises electromagnetic docking across the full LEO temperature envelope by means of a hybrid permanent‑magnet/trim‑coil actuator with passive phase‑change thermal buffering. Fifth, a full theoretical engineering closure is provided, resolving all previously open challenges through conservative first‑principles analysis and reference to existing literature: airtightness via compressive fluorocarbon seals with atomic‑oxygen‑resistant coatings or an internal self‑healing bladder; multi‑material structural welding by autonomous electron‑beam and ultrasonic methods; active radiation shielding through a circulated water jacket; a self‑configuring distributed environmental control and life support network; replaceable battery strategy; crew safety systems; and electromagnetic interference mitigation. Sixth, the strategic AI layer is equipped with a formal natural‑language‑to‑temporal‑logic translation pipeline based on large language models and iterative verification, enabling mathematically guaranteed correctness of all assembly plans. Seventh, the mathematical results are translated into a complete hardware architecture with closed mass, power, propellant, thermal, and economic budgets, demonstrating that the entire programme—from first launch to crewed occupancy—can be realised for under $250 million, approximately 0.16% of the International Space Station's cost for comparable habitable volume.

This dissertation is a work of pure theory and design specification. It contains no experimental data, no trained models, and no prototype test results. Its contribution is a self‑contained, mathematically verified foundation upon which future simulation studies, hardware development, and orbital flight experiments can be built.

