Chapter 1: Introduction

The dominant paradigm of space habitat construction—launching a handful of massive, pre‑integrated modules on heavy‑lift vehicles and connecting them through astronaut extravehicular activity—has remained fundamentally unchanged since the first modules of the International Space Station were mated in 1998 (Kitmacher, 2006). The ISS stands as a monumental engineering achievement, yet its architecture embodies intrinsic limitations: each module is a bespoke structure whose maximum dimension is constrained by launch vehicle fairing diameter; the loss of a single module during a launch failure can delay the entire programme by years; and the reliance on human EVA for mechanical, electrical, and fluid connections imposes severe scheduling, risk, and training overheads (Stockman et al., 2010).

In the decades since, a fundamentally new technological landscape has emerged. CubeSats and SmallSats in the 1–12 U range now offer substantial on‑orbit capability—cold‑gas and electric propulsion, arc‑second attitude control, inter‑satellite optical links, and on‑board artificial intelligence—at costs that are orders of magnitude lower than traditional spacecraft (Swartwout, 2013). Standardised docking and refuelling interfaces, most notably Orbit Fab's Rapidly Attachable Fluid Transfer Interface (RAFTI), have been developed and are approaching flight qualification (Orbit Fab, 2024). Autonomous electron beam welding of structural alloys in vacuum has been successfully demonstrated in orbit by ThinkOrbital, and ultrasonic welding of carbon‑fibre‑reinforced thermoplastics has been adapted for space environments (TWI, 2024; Agile Ultrasonics, 2025). Research groups at MIT's Space Exploration Initiative, the European Space Agency's Advanced Concepts Team, and NASA's ARMADAS programme have begun exploring the proposition that large space structures need not be launched in one piece but can instead be assembled on‑orbit from small, mass‑produced components by autonomous robots (Ekblaw, 2020; NASA, 2023; ESA ACT, 2021).

What has not existed until now is a unified, mathematically rigorous, and architecturally comprehensive blueprint for a human‑rated orbital habitat assembled entirely by an autonomous swarm of small satellites. The closest existing concept—the TESSERAE self‑assembling tile system developed by Ekblaw at MIT—has demonstrated on‑orbit docking of small tiles, but the developers acknowledge that the tiles are not yet airtight and lack the propulsion required for autonomous free‑flying assembly without a containment structure. Subsequent development by Rendezvous Robotics has introduced clamping and sealing mechanisms, but a complete airtight solution and propulsion integration remain an active engineering challenge.

This dissertation fills that gap. Project Archimedes establishes the first complete theoretical and design framework in which a pressurised habitat with 400 m³ of habitable volume is born in orbit from the collective, autonomous behaviour of approximately 500 identical, intelligent building blocks. No heavy‑lift vehicle, no monolithic module, and no astronaut EVA is required at any stage of assembly. All critical engineering obstacles identified in prior art are overcome through a combination of novel hardware designs and mathematical advances, producing a closed, verifiable, and economically viable system.

1.1 Research Objectives

The dissertation is organised around seven research objectives:

1. Formal modelling: Develop a unified graph‑theoretic representation of a habitat under construction that simultaneously captures mechanical connectivity, communication constraints, and fluid‑transfer pathways.
2. Scalable robust synthesis: Create a co‑design algorithm for task assignment and collision‑free trajectory generation that scales to hundreds of heterogeneous free‑flying agents and degrades gracefully under packet loss, with a complete convergence proof.
3. Uncertainty management: Formulate the stochastic assembly process as a Dec‑POMDP and develop a decentralised planning algorithm that exploits graph neural network policy priors and hierarchical decomposition to guarantee probabilistic completeness for 500 agents within bounded computational complexity.
4. Safety‑guaranteed control: Derive a hierarchical suite of nonsmooth control barrier functions that simultaneously maintain algebraic connectivity, avoid collisions, and enforce docking approach safety, incorporating temperature‑compensated electromagnetic actuation with passive thermal management and EMI mitigation.
5. Engineering closure: Resolve all remaining hardware challenges—airtightness, propulsion, welding, atomic oxygen and MMOD protection, radiation shielding, battery lifecycle, fluid interface reliability, distributed ECLSS, and crew safety—through conservative first‑principles budgets and reference to the state of the art.
6. Formal verification of strategic AI: Design a pipeline that translates natural‑language assembly goals into Linear Temporal Logic specifications verifiable by a model checker, closing the semantic gap between human intent and autonomous execution.
7. Complete architecture specification: Translate all results into a fully specified system architecture and position the work relative to TESSERAE and other contemporary concepts.

1.2 Scope

This dissertation is a work of theoretical design. It contains no experimental data, no software code, and no prototype test results. Its contribution is a self‑contained, mathematically verified foundation upon which future simulation studies, hardware development, and orbital flight experiments can be built.

