Chapter 2: Literature Review and Competitive Landscape

2.1 Modular Space Structures and the ISS Legacy

The International Space Station (ISS) is the archetypal modular orbital assembly. Over approximately 40 dedicated launches, modules of 10–20 tonnes each were mated in a carefully choreographed sequence using the Space Shuttle's robotic arm and extensive astronaut EVA (Kitmacher, 2006). The ISS's Common Berthing Mechanism provides a standardised mechanical, power, and data interface, and the station has proven that on‑orbit assembly of large pressurised volumes is feasible. However, the ISS architecture also illustrates the fundamental constraints of the monolithic‑module paradigm: each module is a multi‑billion‑dollar, bespoke structure; module diameter is capped at approximately 4.5 m by the launch vehicle fairing; and the programme's total cost exceeded $150 billion (NASA OIG, 2018).

2.2 Self‑Assembling and Reconfigurable Space Systems

The ESA Advanced Concepts Team (ACT) has studied self‑configuring modular cubes for space applications, formulating the problem as a graph‑based self‑assembly task in which identical modules use local rules to achieve a target configuration. Their work specifically identifies that decentralised control is possible only if the communication graph remains connected, a finding that directly motivates our emphasis on algebraic connectivity maintenance.

NASA's ARMADAS (Automated Reconfigurable Mission Adaptive Digital Assembly Systems) programme has demonstrated the assembly of large truss structures by inchworm‑like walking robots in ground tests and is targeting in‑space demonstrations. ARMADAS proves that kilometre‑scale support frameworks can be built from small mass‑produced voxels, but does not address pressurised volumes or fluid handling.

2.3 TESSERAE and Its Evolution

The TESSERAE concept, invented by Dr. Ariel Ekblaw, is the closest existing work to Project Archimedes. TESSERAE tiles are flat‑packed, self‑assembling hexagons or pentagons that use custom electropermanent magnets (EPMs) to dock and form geodesic shells in microgravity. TESSERAE has achieved notable flight heritage: parabolic flight testing (2017), Blue Origin New Shepard suborbital flight (2019), two ISS orbital demonstrations (2020, 2022), and a planned 32‑tile assembly test on the ISS in 2026. The technology is now being commercialised by Rendezvous Robotics, which raised $3 million in pre‑seed funding in September 2025 and has announced the development of active clamping and sealing interfaces. However, TESSERAE's publicly acknowledged limitations include: (1) the tiles are not yet human‑rated airtight; (2) the tiles currently lack propulsion, relying solely on magnetic attraction within a containment structure; and (3) the published work does not include a formal mathematical framework for stochastic assembly planning, algebraic connectivity maintenance, or temperature‑compensated docking stability. The present dissertation builds upon the TESSERAE heritage and provides the missing theoretical and engineering foundations.

2.4 Swarm Robotics, Distributed Assignment, and Communication‑Robust CBBA

The Consensus‑Based Bundle Algorithm (CBBA), introduced by Choi et al. (2009), provides provable convergence and 50% optimality for distributed task allocation when the score function satisfies diminishing marginal gain (DMG). Recent work has extended CBBA to handle budget constraints, dynamic task sets, and heterogeneous agent capabilities. Importantly, the event‑driven CBBA (ECBBA) of Gupta et al. (2025) addresses the original algorithm's sensitivity to continuous communication by introducing a triggering mechanism that maintains performance under packet loss and delay. In the present work, we adopt an enhanced CBBA with explicit retransmission and acknowledgement, and we prove that convergence is preserved under a bounded packet error rate via a stochastic approximation argument.

2.5 Algebraic Connectivity and Nonsmooth Control Barrier Functions

The algebraic connectivity of a graph, \lambda_2(L), is the second smallest eigenvalue of its Laplacian and characterises how well‑connected the graph is (Fiedler, 1973). Ong et al. (2023) developed a nonsmooth control barrier function framework that uses \lambda_2 as a CBF to maintain connectivity in multi‑robot systems. A key challenge with multiple CBFs—simultaneous feasibility—has been addressed by the hierarchical CBF architecture of Ames et al. (2024) and the feasibility‑guaranteed safety‑critical control method of Liu et al. (2024), which use an offline‑computed priority ordering and adaptive parameterisation to ensure a non‑empty safe control set. We incorporate these advances to simultaneously enforce connectivity, collision avoidance, and docking constraints.

2.6 Decentralised MCTS for Large‑Scale Multi‑Agent Planning

Decentralised Monte Carlo Tree Search with decision sharing and prediction (Dec‑MCTS‑SP), introduced by Cai et al. (2019), has been successfully applied to multi‑robot exploration with up to 10 agents. Scaling to 500 agents requires a structural decomposition. The HIPPO‑MAT framework (2024) demonstrates that graph neural network policies can serve as powerful rollout priors for multi‑agent MCTS, drastically reducing the required search depth. Moreover, recent hierarchical Dec‑MCTS formulations (Zhang et al., 2024) achieve tractable scalability by partitioning the swarm into clusters and planning at two levels. We adopt and formalise this hierarchical GNN‑augmented Dec‑MCTS‑SP, providing probabilistic completeness and a complexity bound that demonstrates feasibility on ARM Cortex‑R5‑class hardware.

2.7 Electromagnetic Docking, Thermal Management, and EMI

Permanent magnet docking offers passive capture but suffers from temperature‑dependent flux and electromagnetic interference generation. The hybrid permanent‑magnet/trim‑coil actuator developed by the DLR iBOSS project (2022) demonstrated stable docking control across a wide temperature range by actively compensating flux variations. To manage trim‑coil resistive heating, phase‑change material (PCM) thermal buffers have been analysed for CubeSat applications, showing the ability to absorb burst heat loads while maintaining component temperatures within safe limits (Kang, 2022). For EMI, the AMODS project (NASA, 2024) has introduced field‑cancelling coil geometries and magnetic shields that reduce stray fields at sensitive avionics by over 30 dB. We combine these concepts into a theoretical Archimedes docking pad.

2.8 In‑Orbit Welding of Multi‑Material Structures

ThinkOrbital and TWI demonstrated autonomous electron beam welding (EBW) of aluminium in space in 2024. For CFRP‑metal interfaces, robotic ultrasonic welding of carbon‑fibre‑reinforced thermoplastics (CFRTP) offers an alternative that avoids the vapour‑deposition and vacuum‑chamber complexities of EBW. Agile Ultrasonics has developed a robotic end‑effector for CFRTP welding that operates in vacuum and can be monitored in situ via acoustic emission sensors. We baseline a dual‑mode welding system that uses EBW for metallic truss nodes and ultrasonic welding for CFRTP‑to‑metal brackets, with X‑ray diffraction and acoustic emission for non‑destructive evaluation.

2.9 Pressurised Bladder and Sealing Technologies

Airlock‑grade seals for segmented structures have been proposed using a compressible Viton gasket with an embedded spring‑energised metallic C‑ring for atomic oxygen protection (Rendezvous Robotics, 2025). For the bladder path, the Bigelow Expandable Activity Module (BEAM) has proven long‑term bladder integrity on ISS. MMOD protection of inflatable bladders is advanced by self‑healing materials: NASA Langley's multi‑layered self‑healing system utilises a polymer that melts upon hypervelocity impact and flows to seal the hole, tested for projectiles up to 1 mm (Ho, 2023). Supramolecular elastomers like Reverlink® have shown autonomous sealing of punctures in laboratory conditions. We incorporate a hybrid Whipple shield on the outer tile surface and a double‑bladder design with an internal self‑healing layer and an outer Vectran restraint.

2.10 Atomic Oxygen and UV Protection

POSS‑enhanced epoxy coatings reduce atomic oxygen erosion by 50% and UV degradation by 80% based on MISSE flight data. For the external tile skin, we baseline a vapour‑deposited multilayer barrier of poly(p‑xylylene) and diamond‑like‑carbon (DLC), which provides integrated AO, UV, and moisture protection with an erosion yield of less than 10^{-26} cm³/atom (Yang et al., 2023). Seals are protected by a thicker DLC overcoat.

2.11 LLM‑to‑Temporal‑Logic Translation

The gap between natural‑language goals and formal verification has been bridged by recent frameworks: nl2spec (Cosler et al., 2023) uses an LLM to translate English sentences to Linear Temporal Logic (LTL) with interactive refinement, while ReasonSTL (Chen et al., 2024) decomposes the problem into syntactic and semantic reasoning steps and uses a tool‑augmented LLM to produce Signal Temporal Logic (STL) formulas with high accuracy. We design a similar architecture for the Architect AI: a pre‑trained large language model is instructed to generate LTL specifications, which are then verified by the SPIN model checker; failed outputs trigger an interactive repair loop that guarantees safe plans.

2.12 Identified Gaps and How They Are Closed

No existing work simultaneously addresses all of the following: a pressurised, human‑rated volume assembled entirely from sub‑15 kg free‑flying tiles; a complete mathematical treatment including stochastic docking, thermal effects on electromagnets, algebraic connectivity under failure, and multi‑CBF feasibility; a scalable decentralised planning algorithm for 500 agents with convergence and complexity guarantees; a closed architecture that resolves airtightness, propulsion, welding, MMOD/radiation shielding, thermal management, EMI, battery degradation, RAFTI reliability, distributed ECLSS, and crew safety; and a formal AI pipeline from natural language to verified temporal logic. Project Archimedes is the first framework to integrate all these elements, and every previously identified engineering gap is closed theoretically by drawing on existing literature and conservative analysis.

