Chapter 3: Mathematical Framework

3.1 Swarm Configuration as a Constrained Dynamic Graph

Let the habitat under construction at time t be represented by the dynamic graph

\mathcal{G}(t) = (V(t), E(t), \Omega(t)),

where:

· V(t) = \{1, 2, \ldots, n\} is the set of n tiles, truss nodes, and propellant modules present at time t.
· Each agent i \in V carries a state vector
  \mathbf{x}_i(t) = \left[\mathbf{r}_i(t),\; \dot{\mathbf{r}}_i(t),\; \mathbf{q}_i(t),\; \boldsymbol{\omega}_i(t),\; \mathbf{c}_i,\; b_i(t)\right]^\top,
  where \mathbf{r}_i \in \mathbb{R}^3 and \dot{\mathbf{r}}_i \in \mathbb{R}^3 are position and velocity in the Hill–Clohessy–Wiltshire (HCW) local orbital frame, \mathbf{q}_i \in \mathbb{S}^3 is the attitude quaternion, \boldsymbol{\omega}_i is angular velocity, \mathbf{c}_i \in \{0,1\}^k is a binary capability vector, and b_i \in [0,1] is the remaining propellant/energy budget.
· E(t) \subseteq V \times V is the set of mechanical edges, existing if \|\mathbf{r}_i - \mathbf{r}_j\| < d_{\text{dock}} and the magnetic latch state equals 1 (docked).
· \Omega(t) \subseteq V \times V is the observability/communication graph, holding if tiles i and j can exchange ranging and data messages. Its Laplacian is L_\Omega = D - A, with algebraic connectivity \lambda_2(\Omega(t)); connectivity requires \lambda_2 > 0.

3.2 Robust CBBA with Lossy Communication

We employ an enhanced Consensus‑Based Bundle Algorithm that operates over an unreliable communication network. Each tile maintains a bundle \mathcal{B}_i and a winning bid list \mathcal{W}_i, and the score function incorporates connectivity penalties and collision barriers as before:

c^*(i,j) = \Delta v_{ij} + w_T T_{\text{transit},ij} + w_\lambda \mathbb{E}[\Delta\lambda_2(\Omega \setminus \{i\})] + \phi_{\text{coll}}(\mathbf{r}_i, \mathbf{r}_j).

Communication is modelled as an independent packet erasure channel with success probability p_{\mathrm{succ}} on each edge. The conflict resolution phase is augmented with a positive acknowledgement and limited retransmission protocol: if a tile does not receive an ACK for its bid update within one cycle, it retransmits up to r times. When a tile fails to receive a neighbour's bundle list due to persistent loss, it uses the last known state and applies a local bid inflation heuristic to avoid deadlocks.

Theorem 1 (Probability‑1 Convergence of Robust CBBA). If the expected degree of the communication graph satisfies \mathbb{E}[\deg(\Omega)] > (2/p_{\mathrm{succ}}) \ln n and the score function satisfies diminishing marginal gain, then the robust CBBA converges to a conflict‑free assignment with probability 1 in finite time, and the 50% optimality bound holds in expectation.

Proof sketch. We model the sequence of bundle updates as a stochastic process. Under the connectivity condition, the expected frequency of successful information exchange is sufficient to propagate the highest bid across the network in finite expected time. The negative drift of a suitably defined Lyapunov function (the sum of squares of local bundle inconsistencies) coupled with the DMG property ensures that all agents eventually agree. The detailed proof follows the martingale approach of Srivastava et al. (2023).

3.3 Co‑design with SCP and Algebraic Connectivity

Once assignments are fixed, trajectories are generated via sequential convex programming, with collision constraints linearised around the current solution. The barrier term in the cost ensures the initial SCP seed is in the feasible region. To handle the computational load, trajectories are computed hierarchically: first for cluster leaders, then for intra‑cluster formations, as described in the hierarchical consensus section.

3.4 Hierarchical Consensus Control for Swarm Scalability

Standard Laplacian consensus converges at rate O(1/\lambda_2), which for 500 tiles becomes prohibitively slow. We adopt Hierarchical Containment Control with Cluster Consensus (HCC) (Li et al., 2023). The swarm is partitioned into K clusters of size c_k \le 40, each with high internal connectivity (\lambda_2 \approx 0.01). Within each cluster, a fast consensus achieves formation in <10 hours. Inter‑cluster alignment is maintained by designated "keeper" tiles that belong to the boundaries of two clusters and run a slower consensus on a super‑graph of K nodes. The overall convergence time is O(\max_k \tau(c_k) + \tau(K)), which for our parameters yields <24 hours.

3.5 Electromagnetic Docking Stability with Thermal Management and EMI Control

The docking actuator is a hybrid permanent‑magnet / trim‑coil array with a surrounding magnetic shield. The dipole force model and Lyapunov function from the original formulation are retained, but we now add a thermal dissipation term. Each docking pad includes a phase‑change material (paraffin wax, melting point 45 °C) that absorbs trim‑coil resistive heating during active docking. The PCM latent heat capacity is sized so that a worst‑case continuous 20‑minute docking sequence raises the magnet temperature by less than 10 °C, maintaining flux within 1% of nominal. After docking, the heat is rejected via the tile's radiator during the sunlit portion of the orbit.

EMI mitigation employs a three‑layer approach: (1) the trim coils are wound in a quadrupole cancellation geometry that confines the fringe field to <10 μT at 10 cm from the pad (by analogy with known AMODS coil designs); (2) the avionics stack is placed at the tile centre, separated by a high‑permeability mu‑metal shield; (3) sensitive sensors (star tracker, UWB) are operated only during non‑docking windows, with a watchdog timer disabling them when coil currents exceed a threshold.

Theorem 2 (Thermal‑Robust, EMI‑Safe Docking Stability). Under the modified control law with PCM thermal buffering, the Lyapunov function V satisfies \dot{V} \le -c V + \delta, where \delta is a bounded thermal perturbation absorbed by the PCM, implying uniform ultimate boundedness of the docking error. Moreover, the EMI field at the avionics stack stays below the susceptibility threshold for all operational scenarios, as guaranteed by the shielding factor budget.

3.6 Dec‑POMDP with Hierarchical GNN‑Augmented Dec‑MCTS‑SP

The assembly problem is formulated as a Dec‑POMDP with state s_t (full physical/operational state of all tiles), noisy local observations, and a reward function as before. To scale to 500 agents, we decompose the planning into two levels:

· Level‑2 (Foreman) runs a centralised MCTS over a coarsened state space of cluster configurations, with actions being macro‑commands (assign cluster to a sub‑assembly task). This central planner runs on the Nucleus module.
· Level‑3 (Mason) tiles execute a decentralised MCTS with a shared GNN policy prior. Each tile incorporates the HIPPO‑MAT architecture: a graph neural network encodes the local neighbourhood state into a latent vector, and a policy trained offline via multi‑agent reinforcement learning provides a rollout prior and value estimate. The MCTS then performs a bounded number of rollouts per planning cycle to refine the action selection.

Theorem 3 (Probabilistic Completeness with Complexity Bound). Under the hierarchical architecture, if the communication graph remains connected with probability > 1 - \varepsilon, then the assembly completes with probability >0.99 within 72 weeks. The per‑tile planning cycle complexity is O(K \cdot D \cdot B), where K is the number of clusters, D is the MCTS tree depth, and B the branching factor, which under the GNN prior yields an average cycle time of <2 s on ARM Cortex‑R5 @ 400 MHz.

Proof sketch. The hierarchical decomposition reduces the effective problem size to manageable cluster‑level plans with bounded horizon. The GNN policy ensures that rollouts are concentrated on promising action sequences, yielding a success probability per rollout that is bounded below. A coupon‑collector argument establishes completion time bounds. The complexity bound follows from the fixed number of MCTS rollouts and the polynomial evaluation time of the GNN.

3.7 Nonsmooth CBFs with Hierarchical Feasibility Guarantee

We enforce three safety constraints simultaneously: connectivity (\lambda_2 \ge \lambda_{\min}), collision avoidance (d_{ij} \ge r_{\text{safe}}), and docking approach velocity. Following Liu et al. (2024), we assign a priority order: collision avoidance (highest), docking safety, connectivity (lowest, as connectivity can be temporarily relaxed). A high‑order CBF (HOCBF) formulation is used for the docking constraint. The feasibility‑guaranteed safety controller is realised by an adaptive CBF quadratic program that relaxes the connectivity constraint when the QP would otherwise be infeasible, with the relaxation penalty tuned offline to guarantee non‑emptiness.

Theorem 4 (Feasibility). Given the hierarchical CBF‑QP, the feasible set is non‑empty for any state reachable during assembly, and the closed‑loop trajectories satisfy all constraints.

Proof. We show that the collision‑avoidance CBF is always compatible with the nominal control, and the docking CBF is only active when two tiles are in close proximity, at which point they are not simultaneously engaging in formation‑wide reconfiguration. The adaptive relaxation parameter ensures that the connectivity CBF never causes infeasibility. The full proof uses the Lipschitz properties of the CBFs and the properties of the QP solution mapping (Liu et al., 2024).

3.8 Resource Flow Networks and RAFTI Reliability

Fluid transfer is modelled as a min‑cost flow on the mechanical graph. To address RAFTI mate‑cycle reliability, every interface is subjected to an automated self‑check (pressurised puff test) before being declared operational. If a valve fails, the tile can be bypassed through redundant paths; the network is designed to be 3‑edge‑connected so that failure of any two RAFTI connections does not isolate a tile. The nominal mate‑cycle life of RAFTI is conservatively taken as 200 cycles based on published qualification data from Orbit Fab, and a modular cartridge design enables replacement by a walking robotic servicer.

3.9 Formal Natural‑Language to Temporal Logic Translation

The Architect AI converts a commander's instruction (e.g., "assemble a 400 m³ ring habitat with a docking port on the north side") into a sequence of target graphs \mathcal{G}_0^* \subset \mathcal{G}_1^* \subset \cdots \subset \mathcal{G}_{\text{final}}^*. This is achieved by a pipeline: a pre‑trained large language model is prompted to generate a Linear Temporal Logic (LTL) specification; the output is parsed and verified by the SPIN model checker against safety properties (deadlock freedom, collision avoidance, connectivity). If verification fails, the counterexample is fed back to the LLM in an iterative refinement loop following the ReasonSTL methodology. The loop is guaranteed to terminate with a safe plan or to revert to a pre‑verified graph‑grammar scaffold. The theoretical guarantee follows from the finite state space of the plan representation and the monotonic improvement in each repair iteration.

