5.4 Propellant and Power Budgets

Per‑tile: 1.5 kg R236FA cold‑gas, 0.3 kg Xe for Hall thruster. Hall thruster Isp = 1,500 s, efficiency 40%, yielding ∆v = 80 m/s for station‑keeping. Combined with cold‑gas manoeuvring (18 m/s), total mission ∆v margin exceeds requirements by a factor of 3. Leakage is addressed by the 3‑edge‑connected RAFTI network and an automated valve closure protocol upon pressure drop detection.

Power generation: 300 solar panels at 60 W orbit average = 18 kW. Total housekeeping + ECLSS = 17.5 kW. Eclipse storage: 500 tiles × 30 Wh = 15 kWh, sufficient for 35‑minute eclipse at 8 kW load even with 20% capacity degradation. Battery replacement every 5 years via robotic hot‑swap.

5.5 EMI and Thermal Feasibility

Analytical modelling shows the quadrupole coil geometry reduces stray fields at the avionics stack by >30 dB relative to a simple dipole, placing the interference level below typical MIL‑STD‑461 limits. A PCM volume of 20 cm³ per pad supplies 2.4 kJ of latent heat, enough to absorb the resistive heating of a 20‑minute continuous docking burst while limiting temperature rise to <10 °C. Orbital‑averaged radiator area of 0.15 m² per tile is sufficient to reject this heat within one orbit (Aguilar et al., 2024).

5.6 RAFTI Reliability Justification

Published accelerated life data for RAFTI primary seals indicate a B10 life exceeding 1,000 cycles (Orbit Fab, 2024). With a design allowance of 200 cycles per port and an automated puff‑check self‑test, the probability of a seal leak escaping detection is <0.1% per cycle. The 3‑edge‑connected fluid network architecture guarantees that any two valve failures can be isolated without segmenting the network.

