Chapter 6: Discussion

This dissertation has provided a complete theoretical closure for Project Archimedes. All seventeen gaps identified in the initial review—spanning airtight sealing, robust propulsion, scalable planning, thermal/EMI management of electromagnetic docking, multi‑CBF feasibility, formal AI verification, atomic oxygen and MMOD protection, battery degradation, RAFTI reliability, distributed ECLSS, and crew safety—have been addressed through a combination of original proofs, conservative first‑principles analysis, and careful integration of published results from the wider literature.

The work remains, by design, a theoretical construct. No physical prototypes have been built, no flight code has been implemented, and no models have been trained on assembly data. The mathematical proofs stand on their axioms; the engineering budgets stand on published component specifications and physical laws. The primary risk that cannot be fully retired through theory alone is the integration complexity of five hundred autonomous spacecraft operating in close proximity. However, the hierarchical decomposition of control, the multi‑layered safety barriers, and the conservative mass and power margins provide a credible argument that this risk is manageable.

The immediate next steps are clear: high‑fidelity multi‑agent simulation of the full assembly sequence, followed by ground‑based hardware‑in‑the‑loop testing of small clusters, and ultimately a subscale on‑orbit demonstration of ten to twenty tiles to validate the docking, fluid transfer, and welding processes end‑to‑end. The theoretical foundation provided here is sufficient to begin that work.

