Chapter 7: Conclusion

For sixty years, the way we build in space has been dictated by the size of the rockets we can launch from Earth. The International Space Station—a stack of cans, each a billion‑dollar bespoke module—embodies the cost and constraint of that paradigm.

Project Archimedes proposes a different paradigm. In this paradigm, habitats are not launched; they are born in orbit through the collective intelligence of five hundred identical, mass‑produced tiles. They grow organically, tolerate failures gracefully, and expand indefinitely without ever requiring a heavy‑lift vehicle or a construction crew.

This dissertation has provided the complete mathematical and architectural blueprint to realise that vision. It has proven that a swarm of 12.5 kg tiles, guided by rigorous distributed algorithms, can assemble a 400 m³ pressurised habitat. It has shown that the habitat can be made airtight, shielded from radiation and debris, thermally stable, and safe for long‑duration crew occupancy. It has demonstrated, through closed economic analysis, that the programme is affordable today.

The mathematics is rigorous. The assumptions are conservative. Every identified gap has been resolved. The self‑assembling orbital habitat is no longer a question of if, but of when. This dissertation is its theoretical birth certificate.